    "last_attempts_log": [
      {
        "attempt": 1,
        "status": "OK",
        "asr_text": "The alarm has not rung yet. The room is quiet. Then suddenly, you are awake just before it sounds. It can feel as if the body has somehow read the clock. Research suggests a less magical but still surprising explanation. The brain and body keep time through an internal daily system. Light and regular sleep and wake times help set that system. When a waking time is expected, the body may also begin preparing before sleep ends. In a laboratory study, blood levels of ACTH, a hormone, rose about one hour before an expected waking time. Researchers saw this as more than the normal daily rhythm. They said it could be part of a preparation process for the end of sleep.",
